F src/record/propose.ts be19beccd3dc 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Proposing a rule: propose(ctx, {target, text, id?, test?, rule?}) resolves a path to its module through walk (or takes an L: module), refuses an unmapped path, an unknown module, a rule the module already has (live, not retired), a taken or malformed id and a test path with spaces, makes an id from the module and the text's first words (proposalId, unique against the graph's constraints), and appends a K G? record with since:<today> to proposals.ctx. Shared by the CLI (ctx propose) and the MCP tool (propose); never touches an agreed rule. Needs ctx.graph, without importing the CLI.
F src/cli/propose.ts 73be32a95d71 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The ctx propose command line: parses <path or L:module> and the rule's words, --id, --test, --rule, calls record/propose.ts, prints the record written (or JSON with --json) and how a person ratifies it, and exits 1 with the reason when refused.
F src/cli/propose.test.ts d4486d2eea60 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Vitest tests for ctx propose on a temporary repository: a path's module gets a K G? proposal in proposals.ctx that ctx proposals lists, a person's ratify turns it into an agreed rule, refusals (unknown module, duplicate rule, taken or malformed id, a test with spaces), unique ids, and the command line's JSON output.
F src/cli/commands.ts 6307cd46a588 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Routes the lifecycle, runtime and presentation commands to modules loaded on demand, so the hook path never pays for them: init/ratify/pack, gate, hygiene/gc/retire, serve/overlay, bench, next/cards, file/agents/proposals/drop (present.ts) and propose (propose.ts).
F src/cli/main.ts a8e95a31f0ad 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The ctx command line: argument parsing, usage text, and the commands hooks and agents call often (slice, hydrate, why, history, record, card, pending, coverage, doctor, install, hook, mcp). Rarer commands live behind commands.ts so this file stays cheap to load. Exports openFromArgs, needGraph, str and latestSession for the lazily loaded modules; the usage text is what ctx help prints, so new commands and flags (file, agents, proposals, drop, propose, ratify --commit) are listed there.
F src/mcp/server.ts fcc03720de4a 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The ctx MCP server (createMcpServer, served over stdio): the tools agents use instead of reading graph files: hydrate, applies, why, history, slice, slice_patch, coverage, check, record (a decision, through Recorder, logged to the session's observations), propose (a rule for a path's module, through record/propose.ts) and card. Paths are normalised to the repository (worktrees included); each call opens the repository the path belongs to.
F src/mcp/server.test.ts 7fcfed5a41b6 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Tests for the MCP server through a real client: the tool list (including propose), the version it reports, serving a slice, refusing and recording a decision, proposing a rule and refusing the same one twice, and writing a card.
F src/cli/commands.ts e289ec38f4bb 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Routes the lifecycle, runtime and presentation commands to modules loaded on demand through extraCommands(args, env), so the hook path never pays for them: init/ratify/pack (init/cli.ts), gate, hygiene/gc/retire (hygiene/cli.ts), serve/overlay, bench, next/cards (guide.ts), file/agents/proposals/drop (present.ts), propose (propose.ts) and module (module.ts). main.ts calls it from its default case; each target module exports run(args, env) and returns an exit code, and extraCommands returns undefined for a command none of them owns so main can report it unknown. A new lazily loaded command needs its case here and its line in main.ts's usage text.
F src/cli/main.ts 648c2e8cc9ab 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The ctx command line: parseArgs, the usage text, and the commands hooks and agents call often (slice, hydrate, why, history, record, card, pending, coverage, doctor, install, hook, mcp, adopt, link, embed). Rarer commands fall through to extraCommands in commands.ts so this file stays cheap to load. Exports Args, parseArgs, str, readInput, latestSession, openFromArgs and needGraph, which the lazily loaded command modules (init/cli.ts, hygiene/cli.ts, cli/module.ts and others) rely on. The usage text is what ctx help prints, so new commands and flags are listed there: it now names ctx module <L:id> --paths with --in, --name and --delegated --reason, alongside file, agents, proposals, drop, propose and ratify --commit.
F src/core/context.ts 46c46d996db4 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Opens a repository through openRepo: its root (sessionRoot picks the session's worktree when an agent works in a linked worktree of the project, else the project), its graph directory and Graph, and the effective config from loadConfig (defaults, then the graph's config.toml, then ~/.ctx/config.toml). Every setting has a default in defaultConfig; [cards] exclude adds to the default exclusions rather than replacing them, and the defaults cover generated output, lockfiles, docs, configuration and dotfiles, which owe no card and need no read before an edit. [delegate] (the delegated ratifier and the kinds it may ratify) is read only from the repository's own config.toml, never the home one, so delegation is the project's choice; unknown kinds are dropped and the default is guidance only. DelegateKind and DELEGATE_KINDS are guidance, enforced, concepts, retirements and now modules (a module's containment edge), which ratify.ts, hygiene.ts's retire and cli/module.ts check against.
F src/init/cli.ts 01fa7eee5265 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The init, ratify and pack commands, loaded on demand from commands.ts. init runs bootstrap and newRecordsOnly, binds packs, counts conformance and prints or, with --write, writes: a new graph.ctx with the config.toml template (whose [delegate] comment now lists modules) and the instruction block, or on an existing graph puts each new module into graph.ctx through addModule (graph/modules.ts) with its containment edge proposed and its mappings ahead of the broader ones that would claim its paths, appending everything else to proposals.ctx. ratify takes --delegated and --reason for the delegated ratifier, or --commit for the person, which checks the branch and ratifier with commitRefusal before changing anything and then commits only the graph with their trailer; the two are refused together. pack lists, exports or reports conformance.
F src/init/ratify.ts 8cd2d09fc157 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human ctx ratify: ratify() turns proposed records active (G? becomes G, or E with a test; proposed concepts and edges lose their flag), moves them from proposals.ctx into graph.ctx, and records each current violation of a checkable rule as a legacy decision in decisions.ctx. With delegated, the project's [delegate] ratifier acts alone and all or nothing, with no write when anything is outside its may_ratify: kindOf counts a proposed 'in' edge as 'modules', so it may ratify a module's containment edge when modules is allowed, while every other edge stays the person's. Each delegated rule or concept gets a decision by '<ratifier>/delegated' naming the kind and the reason; a delegated edge gets none, since a decision must serve a rule or concept, and is attributed by the delegated trailer alone. Without delegated it prints the person's trailer; init/cli.ts and the tests call it.
F src/cli/module.ts 05a07834b992 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human ctx module <L:id> --paths: gives a set of paths its own module so rules can attach to them and not to everything around them, loaded on demand from commands.ts. It relies on graph/modules.ts: currentModule picks the parent (the module the paths map to now) unless --in names one, moduleProblem refuses a malformed id, an already mapped glob or an unknown parent, and addModule writes the mappings, module and containment edge into graph.ctx. The edge is proposed (walked as the parent until ratified) unless --delegated is given, which is refused unless [delegate] is set, its may_ratify includes modules and --reason is given; then the edge is agreed and the Ctx-Ratified-By '<ratifier> (delegated)' trailer is printed. Refusals go to stderr with exit 1; --json reports module, paths, parent, edge state and trailer.
F src/cli/module.test.ts f09002071a8d 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Vitest end-to-end checks for the modules work, each in a throwaway git repository with its own CTX_HOME: ctx module (through run in module.ts) inserts M lines ahead of the mapping that claimed the paths so mapPath, propose and walk see the new module alone, and a proposed containment edge keeps the parent's rules on its files while an agreed edge wins in Graph.parentsOf. It checks the delegated ratifier adds a module agreed only where may_ratify includes modules, may ratify a proposed 'in' edge through ratify but no other edge, and retires under '<ratifier>/delegated' only within retirements. It also checks refusals (mapped glob, unknown parent, malformed id) and that init --write on a curated graph writes a grown folder's module ahead of the root mapping with a proposed edge. It must keep using real files and git, not mocks.
F src/graph/graph.ts 91ccfe87ee0d 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The Graph class: the active set of a repository's graph, loaded from graph.ctx, decisions.ctx, aliases.ctx, proposals.ctx and cards.ctx in one directory (never the archive), and the GRAPH_FILE, PROPOSALS_FILE and other file-name constants. It gives the lookups the walker, hooks, CLI and init rely on: mapPath (the first mapping whose glob matches, so mapping order decides), parentsOf, conceptsOf, constraintsOn, decisionsOn, decisionsFor, nextDecisionId (random ids so parallel branches never collide) and validate. parentsOf now walks a module's proposed containment edge when it has no agreed one, so a module added by ctx module or init keeps its parent's rules until the edge is ratified, and an agreed edge always wins over a proposed one. Over twenty modules import it, including walker/walk.ts, init/conformance.ts and overlay/server.ts, which walk parentsOf.
F src/graph/modules.ts ed0acac53fd1 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Gives a set of paths its own module in graph.ctx, shared by ctx module (cli/module.ts) and ctx init --write on an existing graph (init/cli.ts). It exports probePath (a path inside every path a glob matches), currentModule (the module a glob's files map to now), moduleProblem (refuses a malformed L: id, no globs, an already mapped glob, an unknown parent or a module in itself) and addModule. addModule must insert each new M line before the first existing M line whose glob would shadow it, or after the last M line, because Graph.mapPath takes the first match; it appends the L record and the 'in' edge only when the module is new, the edge proposed with since unless agreed. It relies on Graph, formatRecord and picomatch.
F src/hygiene/cli.ts cb6f3b299576 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human The hygiene, gc and retire commands, loaded on demand from commands.ts: it opens the repository with openFromArgs and needGraph from main.ts and calls hygieneReport, gc and retire from hygiene.ts. hygiene prints each finding with its evidence and proposal, gc reports archived records and dropped expired proposed edges, and retire requires a target and --reason, takes --succ, and now passes --delegated through to retire so the project's delegated ratifier can retire under its own name. It prints the Ctx-Ratified-By trailer when the retirement needs ratification; with --json it prints the findings or gc result.
F src/hygiene/hygiene.ts 1bee1726e0b5 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Hygiene and evolution of the graph: hygieneReport proposes from evidence (rules overridden in practice, unreachable attachments, dormant modules, missing tests, deleted paths, orphaned bases, expired proposals, superseded ADRs, pack mapping drift, debt trend) and never retires; gc archives only what is already inactive and drops expired proposed edges; timeline merges the archive into a node's history. retire appends a Z record with a reason and now takes { delegated }: the project's delegated ratifier retires under '<ratifier>/delegated', refused when no [delegate] is set, and, when the target needs ratifying (a concept or an enforced rule), refused unless may_ratify includes retirements; the trailer names '<ratifier> (delegated)'. It is used by hygiene/cli.ts, main.ts (history --timeline), cli/guide.ts, cli/present.ts and overlay/server.ts.
F src/init/bootstrap.ts 54ad7e1dd2a1 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Bootstrap (design spec §16): derives a proposed graph from the tree with bootstrap(ctx, opts), modules and mappings from directories of source files, dependency edges from the import index, guided constraints from architecture and negative tests and instruction files, header rationale as notes, and concepts from ADRs; newRecordsOnly keeps only records the graph lacks, so re-running proposes additions. On a curated graph (one with any mapping besides **) it doesn't second-guess the existing modules: it proposes a module only for a folder claimed by nothing but the root mapping or a parent folder's /** glob, reuses existing module ids, takes the root id from the graph, and marks each new containment edge proposed with since, skipping dependency edges. init/cli.ts uses it (and writes new modules through addModule), and init.test.ts and module.test.ts check it.
F src/init/init.test.ts bb1a6b4ef08d 2026-10-07 warren.langeveldt/human Vitest end-to-end checks for ctx init's pieces on a seeded throwaway git repository: bootstrap derives modules, containment edges, constraints from architecture tests and instruction files, and concepts from ADRs; it names colliding leaves by their enclosing module, treats src as a convention, and keeps header rationale as notes. On a curated graph it leaves the existing modules alone, proposes modules only for folders the root mapping claims, with every containment edge proposed and dated, and attaches new rules to the curated modules. It also binds the ports-and-adapters pack, counts violations with violationsFor, ratifies with legacy decisions and the person's trailer, and exports a pack.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/register.mjs 49c42294569d 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod's hooks (Claude Code 2.1.287+): registers /graph (not /context, Claude Code's own, or /ctx, the plugin's ctx skill) and /graph-settings, follows the file an agent last read or edited from tool.call (watch only, with a pass-through catch), reads the ctx CLI's JSON (info, proposals, agents, file, settings) and refreshes within 2 s of a tool call or a .ctx file change, draws the Context pane, the Drop dialog, the Settings view (views/settings.mjs) and a band line it stacks above whatever else the band holds, runs the person's acts (ratify --commit, drop --reason --commit, settings set --reason --via pane) only from its own buttons, and asks the lead for cards (askForCards) as a prompt the lead may do itself or hand on. Never enforces; never imports src.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/register.test.ts 0d05d003993d 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod end to end in claude plugin test (Claude Code 2.1.287+), with the ctx CLI stubbed by its JSON: FILE-1 follow and move within 2 s, FILE-3, FILE-4 opening code-kit's Lanes, FILE-5 no graph and no band, RAT-1/2 evidence and band count, RAT-3 ratify (confirm, protected branch, cancel), RAT-4 drop needs a reason, COV-1/2 live counts, COV-3 marked edits, VIEW-6 /graph-settings (a control per setting, a reason before ctx settings set --via pane, a refused value shown, Cancel).
F src/adapters/core.ts 00194df37087 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The harness-neutral hook engine: records reads, edits and coverage per agent, injects slices and cards before edits, refuses edits that aren't understood (read-before-edit and dependencies), demands owed decisions and cards at turn end, and refuses the person's own acts (ratify with --commit, drop, settings set) from every agent before anything else in PreToolUse.
F src/cli/commands.ts 60cd9a8bae67 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Routes the lifecycle, runtime and presentation commands to modules loaded on demand through extraCommands(args, env), so the hook path never pays for them: init/ratify/pack (init/cli.ts), gate, hygiene/gc/retire (hygiene/cli.ts), serve/overlay, bench, next/cards (guide.ts), file/agents/proposals/drop (present.ts), propose (propose.ts), module (module.ts) and settings (settings.ts). main.ts calls it from its default case; each target module exports run(args, env) and returns an exit code, and extraCommands returns undefined for a command none of them owns so main can report it unknown. A new lazily loaded command needs its case here and its line in main.ts's usage text.
F src/cli/main.ts a17f1c42d5ec 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The ctx command line: parseArgs, the usage text, and the commands hooks and agents call often (slice, hydrate, why, history, record, card, pending, coverage, doctor, install, hook, mcp, adopt, link, embed). Rarer commands fall through to extraCommands in commands.ts so this file stays cheap to load. Exports Args, parseArgs, str, readInput, latestSession, openFromArgs and needGraph, which the lazily loaded command modules (init/cli.ts, hygiene/cli.ts, cli/module.ts, cli/settings.ts and others) rely on. The usage text is what ctx help prints, so new commands and flags are listed there: it names ctx module <L:id> --paths with --in, --name and --delegated --reason, ctx settings [set <key> <value> --reason], alongside file, agents, proposals, drop, propose and ratify --commit.
F src/cli/person-acts.ts abcef338d906 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Recognises the shell commands that are the person's acts on the graph (ratify with --commit, drop, settings set), in command position only, so text that mentions them is not refused. Kept tiny because the hook path loads it on every tool call.
F src/cli/present.test.ts d9c0449055cf 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Tests for what the Context Graph pane shows and the person's acts, driven by real hook calls: FILE-2, FILE-3 (per-agent understanding), COV-1, RAT-1 (evidence), RAT-3 (commit on a branch with the trailer, gate passes; refusals on main and for non-ratifiers), RAT-4 (drop keeps the reason), RAT-5 (agents refused, and mentions are not commands), and VIEW-6's settings set refused from agents with its own words.
F src/core/context.ts 3a8fe3c2e253 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Opens a repository through openRepo: its root (sessionRoot picks the session's worktree when an agent works in a linked worktree of the project, else the project), its graph directory and Graph, and the effective config from loadConfig (defaults, then the graph's config.toml, then ~/.ctx/config.toml). Every setting has a default in defaultConfig; [cards] exclude adds to the default exclusions rather than replacing them, and the defaults cover generated output, lockfiles, docs, configuration and dotfiles, which owe no card and need no read before an edit. [delegate] (the delegated ratifier and the kinds it may ratify) and [harness] (the mod's background agents and side questions, harness.ts) are read only from the repository's own config.toml, never the home one, so they are the project's choice. DelegateKind and DELEGATE_KINDS are guidance, enforced, concepts, retirements and modules (a module's containment edge), which ratify.ts, hygiene.ts's retire and cli/module.ts check against.
F src/tool-adapters/code-kit.ts 6e988389a796 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human What ctx knows about code-kit when it shares the repository: a file's spec requirements, lane and layer from code-kit trace (cached per checkout), a session-start note, the branches code-kit protects (from .claude/code-kit.json, defaulting to main and master), where a person's graph commit must not land, and recordPersonsChange: a harness setting the person changes in .ctx/config.toml (which code-kit protects under its approval ctx) is approved with code-kit approve ctx --reason, marked --via context-graph when made in the pane, so code-kit's log keeps it as theirs when committed.
F src/tool-adapters/index.ts 702cd7b9993b 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Tool adapters: what ctx knows about another tool sharing a repository, kept out of the core. Each is detected per checkout and may contribute file facts, a session note, protected branches, and a way to record a person's change to a graph file it guards (recordPersonsChange); the core only calls toolAdapters, factsFor and toolProtectedBranches and the optional methods, and never names a tool.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/views/settings.mjs e0d6a8dcfd63 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod's Settings view (VIEW-6), pure drawing: each harness setting from ctx settings --json with a control (on/off Select, a model Select that keeps an id-named model among its choices, a typed number for pause_at_percent), its default when changed and its about line, and, once one is chosen, a confirmation asking the person's reason (Change it / Cancel) with the CLI's refusal shown. register.mjs owns the acts: ctx settings set <key> <value> --reason --via pane.
F src/cli/settings.ts 0a72b87e1c72 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human ctx settings: lists the [harness] settings in force with their defaults and about lines (--json for the mod), and settings set <key> <value> --reason [--via pane] changes one as the person's act: validated by harness.ts, written into .ctx/config.toml with withHarnessSetting (the rest of the file untouched), and recorded by any tool adapter that guards the file (code-kit's approval log). The hooks refuse settings set from every agent (person-acts.ts).
F src/cli/settings.test.ts da708a377f1e 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Tests for ctx settings (VIEW-6): the list with values, defaults and about lines; set writing [harness] while keeping the file's other sections and comments, and updating a key in place; refusals for an unknown key, an out-of-range value and a missing reason; code-kit's approve ctx called with the reason and --via context-graph from the pane (a stand-in code-kit CLI); harnessFrom, harnessProblems and withHarnessSetting directly; and settings set recognised as the person's act.
F src/core/harness.ts 6ae4fc4417a0 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Context Graph's harness settings, the [harness] section of .ctx/config.toml: card_writer and curator (background agents, off by default) and side_questions (on), each with a _model (empty for the session's), and pause_at_percent (80; code-kit's pause point wins when it's installed). HARNESS gives each a default, a check and an about line; harnessFrom applies valid values over the defaults, harnessProblems words the wrong ones, parseHarnessValue types a command-line value, and withHarnessSetting edits the TOML text in place, keeping comments and other sections.
F src/cli/person-acts.ts 32bb4afa2da2 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Recognises the shell commands that are the person's acts on the graph (ratify with --commit, drop, settings set), in command position only: the line is split where the shell would, outside quotes and with heredoc bodies taken out, and each simple command is matched from its start, so a commit message, a heredoc or quoted text that mentions one is not refused. Kept tiny because the hook path loads it on every tool call.
F src/cli/present.test.ts 9d46180d8ac4 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Tests for what the Context Graph pane shows and the person's acts, driven by real hook calls: FILE-2, FILE-3 (per-agent understanding), COV-1, RAT-1 (evidence), RAT-3 (commit on a branch with the trailer, gate passes; refusals on main and for non-ratifiers), RAT-4 (drop keeps the reason), RAT-5 (agents refused; mentions in an echo, a heredoc body or a quoted multi-line message are not commands, while a real one after a heredoc or in a subshell is), and VIEW-6's settings set refused from agents with its own words.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/register.mjs 31320ff07370 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod's hooks (Claude Code 2.1.287+): registers /graph (not /context, Claude Code's own, or /ctx, the plugin's ctx skill) and /graph-settings. /graph toggles the Context pane; /graph <path> opens it on that file and pins it there until the person follows the agents again (code-kit's combined view opens files this way). Follows the file an agent last read or edited from tool.call when not pinned (watch only, with a pass-through catch), reads the ctx CLI's JSON (info, proposals, cards, agents, file, settings) and refreshes within 2 s of a tool call or a .ctx file change. Keeps the pane's tab, selection and back stack (VIEW-5): j/k select, Enter opens an agent's unread edit in the File tab, and a ui.close hook makes the person's Escape go back before it closes. Draws the Context pane, the Drop dialog, the Settings view and a band line of the graph's health (views/frame.mjs) stacked above whatever else the band holds; runs the person's acts (ratify --commit, drop --reason --commit, settings set --reason --via pane) only from its own buttons, and asks the lead for cards as a prompt. Never enforces; never imports src.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/register.test.ts c08a8f1aa7fa 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod end to end in claude plugin test (Claude Code 2.1.287+), with the ctx CLI stubbed by its JSON (cards included): FILE-1 follow and move within 2 s, FILE-3, FILE-4 opening code-kit's Lanes, FILE-5 no graph and no band, RAT-1/2 evidence and the band's health line, RAT-3 ratify (confirm, protected branch, cancel), RAT-4 drop needs a reason, COV-1/2 live counts, COV-3 marked edits (on their tabs), VIEW-6 /graph-settings, VIEW-1 the header and band health (amber with a card owed, none when healthy and nothing waits), VIEW-5 tabs on 1-3 and j/k with Enter opening an agent's unread edit, and /graph <path> pinning the pane to a file.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/view.mjs 098a6ea48385 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human What the Context Graph mod draws, as pure functions of the ctx CLI's JSON and the elements $.ui.resolve gives: followedPath (which tool calls move the pane, repo-relative), contextPane (through views/frame.mjs: the health header, tabs File, Proposals and Coverage, the keys), its sections laid out as headed fixed-width labelled rows (file: path and who touched it, card, understood with what's unread, module, rules by kind, decisions, code-kit with Open Lanes, Write card or Update card when the card isn't current, Cards for this module; proposals as bordered blocks with evidence in words and Ratify/Drop, the selected one in a bold cyan border; coverage as a table per agent, the selected one marked, with every edit marked understood or not), and dropPane. No mods API, files or processes, so vitest tests it.
F src/mod/view.test.ts b7a185b0c065 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Vitest tests for the Context Graph mod's pure drawing (adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/view.mjs and views/frame.mjs), with stand-in elements: which tool calls are followed, the file section (card current or missing, rules by mode, chain, decisions, what's unread, code-kit lines only when present), no-graph text, proposals with evidence on their tab, coverage counts and edits made without understanding on theirs, VIEW-1 healthOf's levels, glyphs and text and bandHealth, and VIEW-5's moved and closeGoesBack.
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/views/frame.mjs b99dd9d1d486 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human Panes v2's frame for the Context pane (spec 09, VIEW-1 and VIEW-5), pure: healthOf turns ctx cards --json (fresh, stale, missing), the session's cards owed, the proposals waiting and the flagged rules into a carded share, a level (amber with anything owed, a flagged rule or under 80% carded; red with 5 owed or 3 flagged), a glyph (☀ ⛅ ⛈) and the line; bandHealth gives the band that line only while something waits or the health isn't good; frame draws the header with Settings, the notice, the tabs (File, Proposals, Coverage on 1-3), the tab's body and the keys (j/k, o, Esc's words); DEFAULT_UI, moved (nothing selected until j or k) and closeGoesBack (Escape goes back while the back stack holds somewhere).
F adapters/claude-code/hooks/mod/register.mjs 2b77077d7620 2026-10-10 80552949+warrendeanlangeveldt/human The Context Graph mod's hooks (Claude Code 2.1.287+): registers /graph (not /context, Claude Code's own, or /ctx, the plugin's ctx skill) and /graph-settings. /graph toggles the Context pane; /graph <path> opens it on that file and pins it there until the person follows the agents again (code-kit's combined view opens files this way). Follows the file an agent last read or edited from tool.call when not pinned (watch only, with a pass-through catch), reads the ctx CLI's JSON (info, proposals, cards, agents, file, neighbours, settings) and refreshes within 2 s of a tool call or a .ctx file change. Keeps the pane's tab, selection and back stack (VIEW-5): j/k select, Enter opens an agent's unread edit, a neighbour press opens that file (VIEW-2), and a ui.close hook makes the person's Escape go back (restoring the file it came from) before it closes. Draws the Context pane, the Drop dialog, the Settings view and a band line of the graph's health (views/frame.mjs) stacked above whatever else the band holds; runs the person's acts (ratify --commit, drop --reason --commit, settings set --reason --via pane) only from its own buttons, and asks the lead for cards as a prompt. Never enforces; never imports src.
